Qore AMQP module packages
========================

This is a development snapshot for qore-testing, not a final release.
The runtime contains native AMQP 1.0 bindings, two source/AOT helper modules,
compiler metadata, provider resources and all translation catalogs. The doc
package contains three API references and test examples. No broker is installed
or started by the runtime package.

Build with dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc and the matching Qore 3 SDK. Distribution
libqpid-proton-cpp12-dev supplies the native client. Full, arch-only, indep-only,
nocheck and nodoc profiles work offline; cross builds require a separate host
SDK and are rejected. Fixed qcc partitions and explicit sibling dependencies
support reproducibility, and Qore AOT metadata trailers are preserved.

Build and default installed tests run message conversion and provider unit
checks without an external broker. Four explicit QUnit skips identify missing
broker-dependent suites/sections and TLS fixtures. Broker/TLS fixture variables
are cleared during these checks to avoid contacting an external system. Fresh qcc
tests compile and execute installed message conversion and provider registration.

Integration qualification must additionally run all test/*.qtest against a
private AMQP 1.0 broker, setting AMQP_TEST_URL explicitly. Apache Artemis is
used in upstream CI. Ubuntu's ActiveMQ Classic package lacks its AMQP transport,
and Artemis is not a distribution build dependency; downloaded broker binaries
must remain external test fixtures, never source/binary package payloads.
Use a checksum-verified broker release with disposable credentials and data.
Broker management tests permit an unsupported/empty response; they do not
establish complete management feature support. TLS fixtures additionally
exercise private CA trust, hostname verification and
client-certificate authentication. Other TLS/SASL deployment combinations,
cluster failover and other brokers require separate qualification.

Before general publication, qualify other Debian/Ubuntu releases and
architectures, freeze the Qore ABI and test relevant upgrades. No previous
module package is available for upgrade tests. PPA publication requires the
matching published SDK, source signing and upload.
